an their relationship. *What's the real issue here?* The wife's disappointment isn't about the user's emotions per se, but about her own unmet fantasy. She's projective. The user didn't \" fail\" - they're being asked to perform a role that wasn't theirs to play. * red flags in the scenario*: - Maid of honor pressure (overstepping) - \" Tagging you in posts\" as a script (external validation as benchmark) - \"Pictures... I don't look how she pictured\" ( prioritizing photos over real connection) * Jewish perspective check*: - \" * al t'hi r'sha b'eyn yourself\"* ( don't be wicked to yourself) - forcing inauthentic emotion is self-harm. - \" * kol yisrael arevim zeh bazeh\"* ( all Israel is responsible for one another) - but that's about community support, not performance pressure. - The * ketubah* ( marriage contract) is about mutual respect, not staged reactions. *Final judgment*: User is not the ass. The wife's expectation was based on performative social media, not their relationship. The user's calm presence was valid. The real problem is the wife's external validation seeking and the MOH's interference. *Why this matters*: Marriage isn't a performance. If she's disappointed by \" not being dramatic enough,\" that's a deeper issue about whether she values their authentic connection or a viral moment. *Answer structure*: - Clear \" not the ass\" verdict - Break down why expectations were unreasonable - Highlight the performance pressure as the real problem - Note that emotional authenticity > forced tears - Suggest this is a conversation about boundaries, not \" being an ass\"",
